2) Pascal's Triangle I
I am writing here  only how to generate full pascal triangle , other types are indirectly included in it

Brute Force Approach: jo ncr function(jo naam hai wahi kaam hai  har ek element ncr ke formula pe hai) tha usi ko pass kar denge har row ke columns ke liye. 

code: class Solution {
    public int nCr(int n,int r)
    {
        int res = 1;
        for(int i = 0; i<r; i++)   ---> ye loop har ek row ke saari column values ke liye chalega
        {
            res = res*(n-i);
            res = res/(i+1);
        }
        return res;
    }
    public List<List<Integer>> generate(int numRows) {
        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        for(int row = 1; row<=numRows; row++)
        {
            List<Integer> templist = new ArrayList<>();
            for(int col = 1 ; col<=row ; col++)
            {
                templist.add(nCr(row-1,col-1));
            }
            ans.add(templist);
        } 
        return ans;
    }
} 

T.C: in worst case it is near about O(N^3) O(n into n into r)
S.C: we are storing ans , not using to compute , so space will not be considered


Optimal Approach: Usi nCr formula se isko bhi banaye hai 

code: class Solution {
    public List<Integer> generateRow(int row)
    {
        int ans = 1;
        List<Integer> temp = new ArrayList<>();
        temp.add(1);
        for(int col = 1; col<row; col++)
        {
            ans = ans * (row-col);
            ans = ans/col;
            temp.add(ans);
        }
        return temp;
    }
    public List<List<Integer>> generate(int numRows) {
        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        for(int i= 1; i<=numRows; i++)
        {
            ans.add(generateRow(i));
        } 
        return ans;
    }
}

T.C. -> O(N^2)
S.C. -> O(1)

